\section{Sources}{

\itemize{
\item \strong{Data frame} (Stata's varlist, \code{frame()}, \code{if}, and \verb{in}): one column
per variable (\code{vars} picks and orders them) and one row per
observation (\code{subset} keeps some). Character columns are written as
they are (leading spaces included); factors and labelled values
(\code{haven::labelled()}) show their labels, including a label on an
extended missing value (\code{.a}, read by haven as a tagged \code{NA}; a plain
\code{NA} stays blank). \code{Date} and \code{POSIXct} columns use Stata's display
formats: the column's own Stata format when haven kept one (the
\code{"format.stata"} attribute, e.g. \verb{\%tdCCYY-NN-DD}; the common codes
\verb{CC YY NN DD Mon Month HH MM SS ...} are rendered, any other format
falls back to the default), else \verb{\%td} (\verb{06jan2020}) and \verb{\%tc}
(\verb{05jan2020 10:30:00}, in the column's time zone). Logical columns are
written as 1/0 (Stata has no logicals). Other numeric columns show
whole numbers when every value is whole, otherwise \code{digits} decimals; a
value that rounds to zero is written without a minus sign and a
missing value as a blank cell. List, matrix, and data-frame columns
are refused. The header is the column name, or its \code{"label"} attribute
under \code{varlabels}. When the header comes from the labels and the first
row merely repeats them (a table that was already header-shaped), that
row is dropped rather than shown twice (Stata's rule: at least two
rows, every exported column character, every first-row cell equal to
its column's label, the first column optionally blank).
\item \strong{tt_flat} from \code{\link[=tt_flat]{tt_flat()}}: editable regression/effect body records.
The complete schema is validated before selection or writing.
Technical columns (\verb{_order}, \verb{_term}, \verb{_rowtype}, \verb{_block}, and
\verb{_state_<original model index>}) are omitted by default; explicitly
naming them in \code{vars} exports them. Ordinary data-frame columns whose
names start with an underscore remain visible. Only present technical
columns actually omitted by default receive an automatic note; panel
helper columns are excluded separately. Flat body records are never
consumed as an embedded header. Edited visible cells remain literal;
their analytical identity and state remain source provenance.
Row/column \code{[} projections preserve validated metadata. Lost class
with remaining semantic attributes, or malformed metadata, raises
\code{tabtools_error_flat} before any output changes. To deliberately discard
semantics, use \code{data.frame(lapply(flat, identity), check.names = FALSE)}.
Empty row or exported-column selections are refused.
\item \strong{Matrix} (Stata's \code{matrix()}): the row names become the first
(label) column and the column names the header; the label column's
header is blank. Names of the form \code{eq:name} are kept as they are
(Stata's equation stripes) and a leading \verb{_:} (Stata's empty equation)
is dropped; unnamed rows and columns are \code{r1}, \code{r2}, ... and \code{c1},
\code{c2}, ..., as in Stata. Each column is formatted on its own, as above
(\code{digits} applies; \code{varlabels} does not).
\item \strong{tt_table} (R only): the table's cells as its CSV sink shows them,
under one header row flattened as in its Markdown sink
(\code{"Treated (N=1,234)"}, \code{"Model 1: OR"}), or none when the table has
none; \code{title} and \code{footnote} default to the table's own. Its cells are
already text, so \code{digits} and \code{varlabels} do not apply.
}
}

\section{Differences from Stata}{

\itemize{
\item No output file is required: without \code{xlsx} and \code{markdown} (Stata
needs one of them), the table is returned for printing, further
writing (\code{\link[=tt_write_xlsx]{tt_write_xlsx()}}), or \code{\link[=stacktab]{stacktab()}}.
\item R has no frames: a data frame stands for both Stata's varlist of the
current data and its \code{frame()} source, and \code{source} is \code{"data"} for
either (Stata's \code{frame()} gives \code{"frame"}). Stata's \verb{puttab: wrote ...}
line (with \code{xlsx}) and \verb{Markdown exported to ...} line (with
\code{markdown}) are messages.
\item A \code{Date} or \code{POSIXct} column whose Stata format uses a code R does
not render (or an R column without a Stata format) shows the default
\verb{\%td}/\verb{\%tc} display.
\item Flat omitted-key and no-model-span notices are automatic publication
paragraphs after user footnotes in every R sink, and count in \code{n_rows}.
Stata emits these diagnostics only to the console and names its varlist
and variable characteristics. R uses \code{vars} and validated model metadata.
}
}

\examples{
# A matrix: the row names become the label column
fit <- lm(mpg ~ wt + hp, data = mtcars)
m <- cbind(b = coef(fit), se = sqrt(diag(vcov(fit))))
puttab(m, title = "OLS coefficients", digits = 3)

# A data frame, variable labels as the header, written to a workbook
d <- data.frame(term = c("Any HRT", "Former smoker"),
                ahr = c("0.82", "1.14"), ci = c("(0.69, 0.98)", "(0.97, 1.34)"))
attr(d$term, "label") <- "Exposure"
attr(d$ahr, "label") <- "aHR"
attr(d$ci, "label") <- "95\% CI"
path <- tempfile(fileext = ".xlsx")
puttab(d, xlsx = path, sheet = "Block", varlabels = TRUE,
       title = "Primary model", zebra = TRUE)

# Panel variables and source panel headers are excluded automatically
panels <- data.frame(group = c("A", "A", "B"),
                     term = c("Age", "Female", "Age"), n = c(1500, 800, 900))
puttab(panels, panel = "group", panelinline = TRUE,
       panelheader = list(text = c("", "Count")), nformat = "\%12.0fc")

# Edit display cells while retaining model/row identities
flat <- tt_flat(regtab(fit, stats = "n"))
flat[[2]][flat$`_term` == "wt"] <- "See text"
puttab(flat, blockheader = TRUE, footnote = "Edited display cells")
}
\seealso{
\code{\link[=stacktab]{stacktab()}} to assemble puttab sheets into one composite.
}
